cd /tmp/spear-repo

# P1 - Production blockers
gh issue create \
  --title "[P1] Add streaming support — OutputGate is incompatible with streaming LLM responses" \
  --label "enhancement,P1" \
  --body "## Problem

All four gates accept a complete \`string\`. Production LLM deployments almost universally stream responses (OpenAI streaming, Vercel AI SDK \`streamText\`, Anthropic streaming). The OutputGate must buffer the entire response before scanning — eliminating streaming latency benefits and requiring callers to manually accumulate chunks.

## Proposed API

\`\`\`typescript
// New: stream-aware post gate
const guardedStream = spear.postStream(
  readable,        // ReadableStream<string> from your LLM
  { canary }
);

// Passes chunks through in real-time, buffers for scanning,
// throws / closes stream early if a violation is detected mid-stream
for await (const chunk of guardedStream) {
  res.write(chunk);
}
\`\`\`

## Implementation notes
- Accumulate a rolling buffer for canary and n-gram checks
- Flush safe chunks to the consumer immediately
- On violation: emit error event and close stream
- Configurable \`streamBufferMs\` to trade latency for detection accuracy

## Complexity: L" 2>&1 | tail -1

gh issue create \
  --title "[P1] Session memory leak — CanaryManager and toolContexts grow unboundedly" \
  --label "bug,P1" \
  --body "## Bug

Two in-memory Maps grow indefinitely in long-running servers:

1. \`CanaryManager.canaries\` (\`src/core/canary.ts\`) — \`Map<sessionId, canary>\`, never expires
2. \`SpearRuntime.toolContexts\` (\`src/core/runtime.ts\`) — \`Map<sessionId, MediationContext>\`, never expires

In a server handling thousands of unique sessions per day, this is a steady memory leak.

## Fix

Add TTL-based expiry to both Maps:

\`\`\`typescript
class CanaryManager {
  private ttlMs: number = 30 * 60 * 1000; // 30 min default

  generateForSession(sessionId: string): string {
    this.evictExpired();
    // ...
  }

  private evictExpired(): void {
    const now = Date.now();
    for (const [id, entry] of this.canaries) {
      if (now - entry.createdAt > this.ttlMs) this.canaries.delete(id);
    }
  }
}
\`\`\`

## Complexity: S" 2>&1 | tail -1

gh issue create \
  --title "[P1] Add OpenTelemetry trace export — telemetry is trapped in-memory" \
  --label "enhancement,P1" \
  --body "## Problem

\`SpearRuntime.getTelemetry()\` returns events from an in-memory array capped at 1000 entries. Multi-process deployments have no shared telemetry. The policy schema defines anomaly detection thresholds but the anomaly detection pipeline reads zero fields at runtime — it is entirely unimplemented.

## Proposed solution

\`\`\`typescript
import { quick } from '@sunnypatneedi/spear';
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';

const spear = quick('balanced', {
  telemetry: {
    exporter: trace.getTracer('spear'), // drop-in OTel integration
    sampleRate: 1.0
  }
});
\`\`\`

Each gate decision becomes an OTel span with attributes: \`gate\`, \`allowed\`, \`riskScore\`, \`reason\`, \`sessionId\`, \`latencyMs\`.

## Complexity: M" 2>&1 | tail -1

gh issue create \
  --title "[P1] Sidecar has no authentication — oracle attack against cached system prompt embedding" \
  --label "bug,P1,security" \
  --body "## Security issue

\`sidecar/spear_py/api.py\` exposes \`/v1/similarity\` with no authentication. The sidecar optionally caches the system prompt's embedding at startup. Any process that can reach the sidecar port can:

1. Submit crafted text and receive a similarity score against the cached embedding
2. Use oracle queries to gradually reconstruct the system prompt (binary search on similarity scores)

## Fix

\`\`\`python
# api.py — add bearer token check
API_KEY = os.environ.get('SPEAR_SIDECAR_KEY')

@app.middleware('http')
async def auth(request: Request, call_next):
    if API_KEY:
        token = request.headers.get('Authorization', '').removeprefix('Bearer ')
        if token != API_KEY:
            return JSONResponse({'error': 'Unauthorized'}, status_code=401)
    return await call_next(request)
\`\`\`

Require \`SPEAR_SIDECAR_KEY\` in the Docker run instructions.

## Complexity: S" 2>&1 | tail -1

cd /tmp/spear-repo && gh issue create \
  --title "[P4] Fail-closed in enforce mode — gates should block, not allow, on internal error" \
  --label "P4,bug,security" \
  --body "$(cat <<'ISSUE'
## Problem

All gates currently catch internal errors and return `{ allowed: true }` (fail-open). This is correct for shadow mode. In enforce mode, it is a critical security flaw: if a gate crashes (OOM, regex timeout, sidecar timeout), the request is silently allowed through with zero security coverage.

An adversary who can trigger a gate crash bypasses all enforcement.

## Location

`src/core/runtime.ts` — the catch block in `SpearRuntime.pre()` (and `post()`, `tool()`) returns allowed: true unconditionally.

## Fix

Check `this.mode` in the catch block:

```typescript
} catch (error) {
  // Fail-closed in enforce mode: a crashing gate must block, not allow
  if (this.mode === 'enforce') {
    return {
      allowed: false,
      reason: 'Security gate internal error',
      messages: [],
      riskScore: 1.0
    };
  }
  // Shadow mode: fail-open, log the error
  return { allowed: true, messages, riskScore: 0 };
}
```

Same pattern applies to `post()` and `tool()`.

## Tests required

- Mock gate to throw, verify enforce mode returns allowed: false
- Mock gate to throw, verify shadow mode returns allowed: true
- Verify error is logged in both cases (not silently swallowed)

## Complexity: Low

Three catch blocks to update. Tests are the critical part.
ISSUE
)"

